3.3  A register of risks, and the portfolio effect
A company does not face one risk. It faces a list, and the expected losses on that list add — which is one of the few places in risk work where simple addition is correct, because expectations add whether or not the risks are independent.
Figure F803-02 is Orontes's register, reduced to five lines.
Figure F803-02. Orontes's expected loss, five risks
@table
Risk | Probability | Severity | Expected loss
Batch recall | 0.10 | 192,000 | 19,200
Roaster stoppage, seven days | 0.25 | 322,189 | 80,547
Distributor default | 0.05 | 600,000 | 30,000
Fire, sixty days and rebuilding | 0.01 | 6,000,000 | 60,000
Currency loss on export sales | 0.30 | 250,000 | 75,000
Total expected loss | | | 264,747
@end
The arithmetic is four multiplications and an addition: 19,200 + 80,547 + 30,000 = 129,747 for the first three, and 129,747 + 60,000 + 75,000 = 264,747 with the last two. Against operating income of $4,176,000 that is about six per cent, which is a reasonable statement of what risk costs Orontes in a typical year.
Three readings of the table repay attention, and all three are examined.
The ranking by expected loss is not the ranking by danger. The roaster stoppage has the largest expected loss and would not threaten the company; the fire has the smallest but one and could. Expected loss ranks risks by what they cost on average, not by what they could do, which is why Chapter 8's risk map plots likelihood against impact rather than their product.
Expected losses add; dispersions do not. The total expected loss is 264,747 whether the risks move together or not, but the chance of a very bad year depends entirely on whether they do. If the currency loss, the default and the recall all follow from the same economic shock, the portfolio is far riskier than five independent risks of the same sizes.
And the total is only as good as the register. Every number in this table is for a risk somebody wrote down. The loss that ends a company is usually one that was not on the list, which is why Chapter 8's event identification comes before any of this arithmetic.
