LANGUAGE FOCUS   "Expected"
The most misleading word in this chapter is expected. In ordinary English what you expect is what you think will happen; in statistics the expected value is a weighted average that frequently cannot happen at all — Orontes's expected roaster loss of 267,500 is not among the four outcomes. Boards misread the word constantly, and the remedy in a report is to write the sentence out: "in an average year this costs 267,500; in 60 per cent of years it costs nothing; in one year in twenty it costs 800,000 or more." Arabic الخسارة المتوقعة carries the same ambiguity as the English and needs the same explanation; French perte attendue is slightly better, since attendue is closer to awaited than to predicted, and French technical writing often prefers espérance de perte, which names the mathematical expectation explicitly and cannot be misread as a forecast.
