Case-style practice: the maintenance contract
Orontes's engineering manager proposes a maintenance contract on the roaster costing $180,000 a year. The supplier's evidence suggests it would change the four outcomes of section 3.2 to: nothing with probability 0.80, a short stop costing 150,000 with probability 0.15, a serious failure costing 800,000 with probability 0.04, and a destroyed roaster costing 3,000,000 with probability 0.01.
Required. Compute the new expected loss, decide whether the contract pays, and say what else you would consider before recommending it. Under 300 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC3-1  Answer A. Frequency from repeated observation is strongest.
B is wrong. Judgment is used where data are absent.
C is wrong. A model inherits its assumptions.
D is wrong. Industry averages may not fit this company.
SC3-2  Answer A. Exhaustive, mutually exclusive outcomes sum to one.
B is wrong. A shortfall means an outcome is missing.
C is wrong. Equality is not required.
D is wrong. Independence is a different property.
SC3-3  Answer A. The product rule needs independence.
B is wrong. Mutually exclusive events cannot both occur.
C is wrong. Equality is irrelevant.
D is wrong. No distributional assumption is needed.
SC3-4  Answer A. Probability times severity, summed.
B is wrong. That is the maximum possible loss.
C is wrong. The most likely outcome here is zero.
D is wrong. That is a percentile.
SC3-5  Answer A. 37,500 + 80,000 + 150,000 = 267,500.
B is wrong. That adds severities without weighting.
C is wrong. That averages the three severities.
D is wrong. That is one severity.
SC3-6  Answer A. The saving of 133,750 exceeds the 100,000 cost.
B is wrong. The arithmetic says otherwise.
C is wrong. Halving the loss changes the expectation.
D is wrong. Halving is not elimination.
SC3-7  Answer A. Expectations are additive regardless.
B is wrong. Independence is needed for dispersions, not expectations.
C is wrong. Multiplication is for joint probabilities.
D is wrong. They combine straightforwardly.
SC3-8  Answer A. 129,747 + 60,000 + 75,000 = 264,747.
B is wrong. That is the first three only.
C is wrong. That is the last two only.
D is wrong. That adds severities, not expected losses.
SC3-9  Answer A. Average cost is not the same as danger.
B is wrong. Probability is in the product.
C is wrong. So is severity.
D is wrong. It is easily computed.
SC3-10  Answer A. The excess over the expectation at a percentile.
B is wrong. That is the average.
C is wrong. That is the maximum possible loss.
D is wrong. A premium is a price.
SC3-11  Answer A. 800,000 − 267,500 = 532,500.
B is wrong. That is the percentile loss itself.
C is wrong. That is the expectation.
D is wrong. This adds instead of subtracting.
SC3-12  Answer A. Imperfect correlation gives a diversification benefit.
B is wrong. Only perfect correlation gives exact addition.
C is wrong. Multiplication is not involved.
D is wrong. Cancellation would require perfect negative correlation.
SC3-13  Answer A. The worst conceivable outcome, unweighted.
B is wrong. That is an unexpected loss measure.
C is wrong. That is the expected loss.
D is wrong. A premium is a price.
SC3-14  Answer A. Survival outranks likelihood.
B is wrong. Low probability does not make ruin acceptable.
C is wrong. Budgeting the maximum would distort every figure.
D is wrong. Ignoring it is the error the measure exists to prevent.
SC3-15  Answer A. 0.01 × 6,000,000 = 60,000.
B is wrong. That is the severity.
C is wrong. That misplaces a decimal.
D is wrong. That is the excess in the year it occurs.
Your turn
@table
Item | Answer
Do the probabilities sum to one? | Yes: 0.70 + 0.20 + 0.08 + 0.02 = 1.00.
Expected loss | 100,000: 0.20 × 100,000 = 20,000, plus 0.08 × 500,000 = 40,000, plus 0.02 × 2,000,000 = 40,000.
Loss not exceeded in 98 per cent of years | 500,000, since the cumulative probability reaches 0.98 at that outcome.
Unexpected loss at that level | 400,000: 500,000 − 100,000 = 400,000.
Maximum possible loss on this table | 2,000,000, the worst outcome listed.
Which figure decides retention | The maximum possible loss, compared with the company's capacity to absorb it.
What it tells you | One table, three figures, three decisions: what to budget, what to hold capital against, and whether the risk may be kept at all.
@end
Multiple-choice questions
P3-01  Answer A. Probability times severity, summed.
B is wrong. That is the maximum.
C is wrong. Frequency is not expectation.
D is wrong. That is a percentile.
P3-02  Answer A. They sum to one.
B is wrong. The sum is exactly one.
C is wrong. Completeness follows from the sum.
D is wrong. They are perfectly usable.
P3-03  Answer A. 37,500 + 80,000 + 150,000 = 267,500.
